1
00:00:01,860 --> 00:00:09,908
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I had in mind to speak about noble friendship, like what the Buddha says about that and how to cultivate that.

2
00:00:11,329 --> 00:00:15,430
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll start with the kind of the most famous sutta about noble friendship.

3
00:00:15,510 --> 00:00:17,710
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in the Saṃyutta Nikaya.

4
00:00:18,931 --> 00:00:29,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's see... King Pasenadi is visiting the Buddha and he says... Dear Venerable Sir, while I was alone in seclusion, the following reflection arose in my mind.

5
00:00:29,753 --> 00:00:32,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Dhamma has been well expounded by the Blessed One.

6
00:00:33,073 --> 00:00:40,175
[SPEAKER_00]: It is for one with good friends, good companions, good comrades, not for one with bad friends, bad companions, and bad comrades.

7
00:00:41,095 --> 00:00:42,056
[SPEAKER_00]: So it is, Great King.

8
00:00:42,276 --> 00:00:42,737
[SPEAKER_00]: So it is.

9
00:00:43,357 --> 00:00:48,782
[SPEAKER_00]: This dhamma has been well-explained by me, and it is for the good... one with good friends, not for one with bad friends.

10
00:00:49,503 --> 00:00:54,788
[SPEAKER_00]: On one occasion, Great King, I was living among the Sakans, where there's a town named Nagaraka.

11
00:00:56,667 --> 00:01:06,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Abhiku Ananda approached me, paid homage to me, and sat down at one side and said, venerable sir, this is half the holy life, that is, good friendship, good companionship, good comradeship.

12
00:01:07,809 --> 00:01:11,890
[SPEAKER_00]: When this was said, I told Abhiku Ananda, not so, Ananda, not so.

13
00:01:12,470 --> 00:01:18,131
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the entire holy life, Ananda, that is, good friendship, good companionship, good comradeship.

14
00:01:19,152 --> 00:01:26,333
[SPEAKER_00]: When Abhiku has a good friend, a good companion, a good comrade, it is to be expected that he will develop and cultivate the Noble Eightfold Path.

15
00:01:27,715 --> 00:01:33,912
[SPEAKER_00]: And how does a bhikkhu who has a good friend, a good companion, a good comrade, develop and cultivate the Noble Eightfold Path?

16
00:01:34,571 --> 00:01:35,692
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's the thing.

17
00:01:35,712 --> 00:01:43,717
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I'm sure you've heard it before, but I, myself, many, many times, heard that sutta quoted, but only part of the sutta quoted, only the first part.

18
00:02:04,909 --> 00:02:30,760
[SPEAKER_02]: Where it says, and that's quoted by monks and nuns as well, in the talks, saying how the noble friendship is the entirety of the holy life, and then they wouldn't explain how, but instead go on talking about how community, company, camaraderie, in a sense, we're doing things together, we're together, together, together, together, is justifiable by that quote, which it isn't.

19
00:02:31,703 --> 00:02:34,307
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you carry on reading the same sutra, and then the Buddha explains how.

20
00:02:34,367 --> 00:02:36,250
[SPEAKER_02]: It's because you take him on as a friend.

21
00:02:37,131 --> 00:02:46,104
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, with a friendly eye, you regard what he said could be beneficial for you, and out of that faith, in that friendship, you go forth.

22
00:02:46,124 --> 00:02:47,766
[SPEAKER_02]: You never even meet the Buddha, by the way.

23
00:02:48,588 --> 00:02:59,193
[SPEAKER_02]: You just heard about him and you regard him as a friend and you leave your home, become a monk out of that faith, make the effort, get the right view and you verify his teaching without ever meeting him.

24
00:02:59,213 --> 00:03:03,915
[SPEAKER_02]: There's nothing to do with sort of hanging out and spending time together.

25
00:03:03,955 --> 00:03:07,976
[SPEAKER_02]: It's to do basically with having friends that know more than you in terms of the dhamma and then learning.

26
00:03:08,857 --> 00:03:11,238
[SPEAKER_02]: But exactly, it says that it's based on seclusion.

27
00:03:12,158 --> 00:03:16,540
[SPEAKER_02]: What you're going to learn through friendship is based on seclusion, withdrawal, sense restraint and all that.

28
00:03:17,576 --> 00:03:24,622
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's it's it's it's a it's a far cry from from from justifying community group activities and whatnot.

29
00:03:25,583 --> 00:03:30,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there's almost like a distinction, I think, between like sensual friendship, so to say, and this other kind.

30
00:03:31,148 --> 00:03:35,132
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, like the thing is people depend on company more often than not.

31
00:03:35,752 --> 00:03:37,013
[SPEAKER_02]: The solitude is not pleasant.

32
00:03:37,794 --> 00:03:46,681
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, if you're already given into that dependence, and you're ignoring your responsibility for it, you will then just look for anything remotely to justify it.

33
00:03:47,221 --> 00:03:51,645
[SPEAKER_02]: And the first part of that sutta serves that purpose, because you don't read the other bit.

34
00:03:52,265 --> 00:03:55,327
[SPEAKER_02]: It's almost like an abstract friendship that the Buddha talks about.

35
00:03:55,347 --> 00:04:02,193
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you never meet him, you just hear about him, and you know, it's a friendly disposition, and because of that you go forth.

36
00:04:03,439 --> 00:04:12,804
[SPEAKER_02]: But you don't mention that, because that would undermine the purpose of trying to justify, you know, being able to withstand seclusion.

37
00:04:14,188 --> 00:04:20,691
[SPEAKER_00]: It's almost a sense of like you're... it's like... it's like almost like a... intertwined with like faith in that sense.

38
00:04:20,711 --> 00:04:23,572
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, that's why there's a friend... Yeah, that's exactly what he said.

39
00:04:23,672 --> 00:04:29,155
[SPEAKER_02]: It's out of that... Because of that friendship, they... they trust... they develop this trust that, oh, this... this could help me.

40
00:04:30,075 --> 00:04:36,338
[SPEAKER_02]: If they don't regard him with a friendly eye, in that sense, he wouldn't... he wouldn't... you know, he would look for faults.

41
00:04:36,538 --> 00:04:38,599
[SPEAKER_02]: He wouldn't commit and he certainly would not have faith in it.

42
00:04:40,420 --> 00:04:43,822
[SPEAKER_02]: So, basically, what he's saying, whoever wants to help you,

43
00:04:44,536 --> 00:04:48,417
[SPEAKER_02]: If you perceive, oh, this could help me, that's already a form of friendship.

44
00:04:49,197 --> 00:04:50,898
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot not regard him as a friend.

45
00:04:51,898 --> 00:04:56,160
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you don't necessarily know whether it will be successful or not, the promise to help.

46
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:10,464
[SPEAKER_00]: It sounds then like, it's almost like an acknowledgment of a... like fundamental isolation, fundamental selfishness, because it's like, this person's a friend because they can help me, but not in that kind of way that people denigrate either.

47
00:05:10,844 --> 00:05:11,384
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

48
00:05:11,424 --> 00:05:12,444
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no other way around it.

49
00:05:12,484 --> 00:05:14,285
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you will have to start selfishly.

50
00:05:14,816 --> 00:05:18,479
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just a certain type of selfishness can result in cessation of selfishness.

51
00:05:19,140 --> 00:05:22,443
[SPEAKER_02]: And that type of selfishness is practicing the dhamma.

52
00:05:22,543 --> 00:05:25,065
[SPEAKER_02]: That type of craving is craving for the understanding of the dhamma.

53
00:05:25,805 --> 00:05:27,487
[SPEAKER_02]: And... what was the other one?

54
00:05:29,292 --> 00:05:34,534
[SPEAKER_02]: And that type of conceit is the conceit for, you know, being an Arahant, being one of the enlightened beings.

55
00:05:35,255 --> 00:05:40,797
[SPEAKER_02]: But because of that conceit, because of that craving, because of that selfishness, you actually are making the effort in the right direction.

56
00:05:40,817 --> 00:05:44,859
[SPEAKER_02]: They will cancel those very things, when you do it right, eventually.

57
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:47,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

58
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:54,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Other times, with people who delight in company, it always seems to me that it's like almost denying that fundamental need to be selfless.

59
00:05:54,723 --> 00:05:56,484
[SPEAKER_02]: I think it's like one of the worst impediments.

60
00:05:57,384 --> 00:05:58,445
[SPEAKER_02]: Minds, of course, provided

61
00:05:59,248 --> 00:06:00,489
[SPEAKER_02]: Sense of strength is practiced.

62
00:06:00,589 --> 00:06:00,809
[SPEAKER_02]: Yep.

63
00:06:01,589 --> 00:06:02,210
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a must.

64
00:06:02,630 --> 00:06:09,253
[SPEAKER_02]: But then it's the company, careless chatter, just hanging out.

65
00:06:09,714 --> 00:06:10,854
[SPEAKER_02]: There's so many suttas.

66
00:06:11,495 --> 00:06:12,535
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all the world talks about.

67
00:06:13,175 --> 00:06:13,996
[SPEAKER_02]: How to not do that.

68
00:06:14,256 --> 00:06:14,916
[SPEAKER_02]: How to avoid it.

69
00:06:15,016 --> 00:06:17,138
[SPEAKER_02]: Why you should not do it.

70
00:06:17,678 --> 00:06:18,598
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't be negligent.

71
00:06:19,579 --> 00:06:23,481
[SPEAKER_02]: Practice seclusion because... until it's too late to do it.

72
00:06:24,625 --> 00:06:26,746
[SPEAKER_02]: So, eh... so it's not really...

73
00:06:26,766 --> 00:06:28,127
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, he was... he was telling off.

74
00:06:28,767 --> 00:06:46,856
[SPEAKER_02]: The Buddha was telling off to the monks, probably attained monks, obviously not arahants, but, eh... clearly monks on the path, they were just hanging out together, sewing robes, preparing for the kathina, you know, the things that are good, seemingly.

75
00:06:46,876 --> 00:06:51,818
[SPEAKER_02]: He would sort of blast them, like really, really, really sort of serious, serious telling off.

76
00:06:53,636 --> 00:06:56,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's just wasting their time in company.

77
00:06:56,677 --> 00:06:59,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you say, like, who's making noise, like a bunch of fishermen.

78
00:06:59,479 --> 00:07:01,239
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, that's it, that's it, that's it.

79
00:07:01,259 --> 00:07:01,820
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like...

80
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:07,142
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm approaching this monastery and I thought there's, like, a band of fishermen here, selling fish and sharting.

81
00:07:07,722 --> 00:07:10,503
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's the monks, supposedly, practicing.

82
00:07:11,203 --> 00:07:17,366
[SPEAKER_02]: And again, when you... then you read the sutta later, it's not that they were crazy doing... No, they were actually sewing robes for the kathina occasion.

83
00:07:18,507 --> 00:07:19,468
[SPEAKER_02]: And the Buddha told them off.

84
00:07:19,708 --> 00:07:22,890
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, yeah, what's the purpose of your holy life?

85
00:07:24,271 --> 00:07:31,397
[SPEAKER_02]: And, eh... See, it doesn't mean, like, oh, every getting together, like, to justify, like, hating on groups or something like that.

86
00:07:31,497 --> 00:07:32,918
[SPEAKER_02]: No, that's your own personal issue.

87
00:07:33,498 --> 00:07:40,343
[SPEAKER_02]: Because sometimes people don't know how to deal with people, and they try to use solitude to justify... See, the Buddha said we should be in solitude.

88
00:07:40,784 --> 00:07:44,406
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, for practice, not because you hate everybody else, and you can't live with them.

89
00:07:45,467 --> 00:07:47,509
[SPEAKER_02]: Because then you're just in solitude with your own hate.

90
00:07:48,220 --> 00:07:50,903
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you need to discern where your motivation is rooted, as always.

91
00:07:51,724 --> 00:07:54,127
[SPEAKER_02]: And once you do, then you will do the solitude rightly.

92
00:07:55,628 --> 00:08:03,111
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, how, let's say, with that example, the person just hates everyone, it's not that, no, I'm gonna not hate everyone by going to be in company all the time.

93
00:08:03,892 --> 00:08:05,893
[SPEAKER_00]: How would they, like, address that issue?

94
00:08:05,913 --> 00:08:12,055
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you address that issue first by, as I said, by being aware of the motivation behind whether you're solitude or whether you're company.

95
00:08:12,536 --> 00:08:19,619
[SPEAKER_02]: And again, same, more often than not, people who try to justify the company, the community, the group, is people who don't know how to live alone.

96
00:08:20,395 --> 00:08:24,216
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have that side of things, but then, yeah, you have many people who just

97
00:08:45,933 --> 00:08:54,815
[SPEAKER_02]: I have no social or have little social skills, so they cannot bear other people very well and don't know how to relate to it and always results in friction.

98
00:08:55,775 --> 00:08:58,476
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then they go to solitude and quote the suttas about solitude.

99
00:08:58,916 --> 00:09:00,196
[SPEAKER_02]: But where is that rooted?

100
00:09:00,296 --> 00:09:04,177
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it in this their understanding of necessity of solitude as a basis for the practice?

101
00:09:04,617 --> 00:09:04,757
[SPEAKER_02]: Nope.

102
00:09:04,937 --> 00:09:08,238
[SPEAKER_02]: It's rooted in their real will towards those people they don't know how to deal with.

103
00:09:09,298 --> 00:09:11,298
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then you won't be doing it for the right reasons.

104
00:09:11,338 --> 00:09:15,179
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to find... you need to reroute your motivation into the right reasons.

105
00:09:16,001 --> 00:09:20,709
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there is no like, oh, you should then stay with people, or you should then go to solitude.

106
00:09:21,431 --> 00:09:22,733
[SPEAKER_02]: It depends why you do it.

107
00:09:23,334 --> 00:09:25,518
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you do it rightly, you will then go to solitude.

108
00:09:26,913 --> 00:09:29,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is a necessary basis for you to understand.

109
00:09:29,494 --> 00:09:34,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you're in like a place like this, where there are people living together, so to speak, or...

110
00:09:34,455 --> 00:09:44,918
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I suppose, practical advice would be, if you are considering finding a place where you can kind of try a bit of both, that's what you need to look for.

111
00:09:45,398 --> 00:09:52,540
[SPEAKER_02]: The place that would then allow you, if possible, to be in solitude, but then also have some reference point of other people.

112
00:09:53,463 --> 00:09:59,405
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you make sure you don't give in just... and whatever you're doing is rooted in the wrong reasons.

113
00:09:59,425 --> 00:10:00,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

114
00:10:00,065 --> 00:10:05,206
[SPEAKER_00]: It seems valuable to me to be able to have yourself exposed to different situations.

115
00:10:05,446 --> 00:10:06,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, of course.

116
00:10:06,306 --> 00:10:09,207
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I think that's the whole idea of nisaya in the beginning.

117
00:10:09,647 --> 00:10:17,610
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, having somebody to learn from means you're not completely on your own until you can be completely on your own.

118
00:10:20,005 --> 00:10:27,668
[SPEAKER_02]: But, again, people who already decided that they don't want the solitude, then they would use this to justify the company in the group.

119
00:10:27,768 --> 00:10:32,030
[SPEAKER_02]: When the Buddha said, you need to be prepared for it, like, you know, living in the wilderness dwellings.

120
00:10:32,650 --> 00:10:36,772
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to have the right view, established in virtue, sense restraint, all these things.

121
00:10:37,893 --> 00:10:40,794
[SPEAKER_02]: And then they would say, okay, so, see, you must be ready for the solitude.

122
00:10:40,814 --> 00:10:43,395
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so, is the company making you ready for the solitude?

123
00:10:44,276 --> 00:10:44,996
[SPEAKER_02]: How is that possible?

124
00:10:45,696 --> 00:10:48,737
[SPEAKER_02]: Hanging out with people is preparing me to be alone.

125
00:10:48,998 --> 00:10:49,638
[SPEAKER_02]: How does that work?

126
00:10:50,187 --> 00:10:50,567
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't.

127
00:10:52,069 --> 00:10:54,270
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's a completely separate decision that you make.

128
00:10:54,731 --> 00:10:58,774
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, I'm not ready for the solitude, but it doesn't mean I'm justified for company.

129
00:10:59,474 --> 00:10:59,614
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

130
00:11:00,335 --> 00:11:01,736
[SPEAKER_02]: It just means I'm not ready for the solitude.

131
00:11:02,497 --> 00:11:07,821
[SPEAKER_02]: So, make yourself ready for the solitude, through wisely using company and learning from it.

132
00:11:10,963 --> 00:11:13,866
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the rest of that sutta is, as you mentioned,

133
00:11:17,113 --> 00:11:22,916
[SPEAKER_00]: By following the following method too, Ananda, it may be understood how the entire holy life is good friendship.

134
00:11:23,736 --> 00:11:39,545
[SPEAKER_00]: By relying on me as a good friend, Ananda, being subject to birth or freed from birth, being subject to aging or freed from aging, subject to illness or freed from illness, subject to death or freed from death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, grief and despair are freed from those things.

135
00:11:40,459 --> 00:11:45,901
[SPEAKER_00]: By this method, Ananda, it may be understood how the entire holy life is good friendship.

136
00:11:48,442 --> 00:11:51,443
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he goes on to say further, further exhortation.

137
00:11:51,503 --> 00:11:53,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, great king, you should train yourself thus.

138
00:11:54,465 --> 00:11:57,826
[SPEAKER_00]: I will be one who has good friends, good comrades, good friendship.

139
00:11:58,886 --> 00:12:02,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you have good friends, you should dwell with one thing for support.

140
00:12:02,968 --> 00:12:04,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Diligence and wholesome states.

141
00:12:05,777 --> 00:12:14,339
[SPEAKER_00]: He goes on to say that you should also exert your... all your people do the same and they're all gonna... that'll have... be for your mutual benefit and welfare and so on.

142
00:12:15,640 --> 00:12:15,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

143
00:12:15,880 --> 00:12:26,162
[SPEAKER_02]: So, basically, the good friend then is defined by the one that will... whose presence, interaction with, will result in your wisdom increasing.

144
00:12:26,883 --> 00:12:32,264
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not defined by the one that makes me feel good, or makes me feel happy, or makes me feel content.

145
00:12:33,010 --> 00:12:35,411
[SPEAKER_00]: You could even be someone who does not make you feel very happy.

146
00:12:35,451 --> 00:12:37,232
[SPEAKER_00]: More often than not, I think that's the case.

147
00:12:37,372 --> 00:12:38,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

148
00:12:38,412 --> 00:12:40,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Like someone who's poking you in the way you don't want to be poked.

149
00:12:40,833 --> 00:12:45,735
[SPEAKER_02]: But you're not acting from the point of view of your precious feelings.

150
00:12:46,395 --> 00:12:48,716
[SPEAKER_02]: You're acting out of recognition what's good for you.

151
00:12:49,837 --> 00:12:59,361
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the rhinoceros poem, when it said, unless you find a friend better or at least equal, he's no friend of yours then.

152
00:12:59,821 --> 00:13:00,541
[SPEAKER_02]: So you should leave.

153
00:13:00,894 --> 00:13:03,455
[SPEAKER_02]: You should not hang with those who are inferior to you.

154
00:13:03,475 --> 00:13:11,599
[SPEAKER_02]: And it refers to the point of view of understanding and virtue and development of wisdom.

155
00:13:11,779 --> 00:13:13,460
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's an important thing to keep in mind.

156
00:13:13,480 --> 00:13:16,921
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, yeah, you'll just be, oh, you know, he's a good mate.

157
00:13:17,662 --> 00:13:20,183
[SPEAKER_02]: I like his company, hanging out, whatever else.

158
00:13:21,563 --> 00:13:22,584
[SPEAKER_02]: So he's a good friend, you see.

159
00:13:23,484 --> 00:13:24,425
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not what it means.

160
00:13:29,193 --> 00:13:31,075
[SPEAKER_00]: There's another sutta that goes into exactly that.

161
00:13:31,095 --> 00:13:34,899
[SPEAKER_00]: It's from, in the Gujarat Book of Eights, the sutta to Dighajanu.

162
00:13:36,039 --> 00:13:39,100
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just read the part that goes on the good friendship.

163
00:13:40,420 --> 00:13:43,981
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha tells this guy, what is good friendship?

164
00:13:44,522 --> 00:13:46,862
[SPEAKER_00]: This is for a householder, but I think it applies to anyone.

165
00:13:47,562 --> 00:13:53,444
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a case where a householder spends time with householders or their sons, young or old, accomplished in virtue.

166
00:13:54,044 --> 00:13:57,145
[SPEAKER_00]: He speaks with them, engages them in discussion.

167
00:13:57,725 --> 00:13:59,606
[SPEAKER_00]: He emulates that accomplishment in virtue.

168
00:14:00,226 --> 00:14:02,987
[SPEAKER_00]: And likewise, with faith, generosity, and wisdom.

169
00:14:04,027 --> 00:14:12,531
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, as he's saying there, it's like, the whole point is to, like, kind of, pick up the signs, almost, of, like, what does it mean to be like this, and then you can see them in yourself.

170
00:14:12,551 --> 00:14:17,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, which means you already need to be, internally, valuing virtue for yourself.

171
00:14:17,954 --> 00:14:19,515
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, you wouldn't pursue these people.

172
00:14:19,535 --> 00:14:23,457
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't see them as valuable, and exactly, then you wouldn't have a discernment for the pursuit.

173
00:14:24,237 --> 00:14:27,939
[SPEAKER_02]: But you want, so you recognize that virtue is worth being developed.

174
00:14:29,460 --> 00:14:31,661
[SPEAKER_02]: And you, you know, you might try, partially,

175
00:14:32,657 --> 00:14:35,418
[SPEAKER_02]: Kind of successful, maybe not as much as you would want to.

176
00:14:35,458 --> 00:14:36,598
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how can I improve this?

177
00:14:37,039 --> 00:14:39,159
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, well, let me find those who are more virtuous than me.

178
00:14:40,220 --> 00:14:41,360
[SPEAKER_02]: And let me learn that from them.

179
00:14:42,681 --> 00:14:44,201
[SPEAKER_02]: And let me emulate it.

180
00:14:44,481 --> 00:14:45,902
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I'll be as virtuous as they are.

181
00:14:46,322 --> 00:14:48,263
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you benefited from that type of friendship.

182
00:14:49,543 --> 00:14:51,444
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just friendship in order to do the work.

183
00:14:52,304 --> 00:14:54,025
[SPEAKER_02]: Not friendship in order to make yourself feel good.

184
00:14:58,452 --> 00:15:04,916
[SPEAKER_00]: And the last sutta I had is an interesting example of, like, this is a kind of like the example of a quote-unquote perfect community.

185
00:15:05,737 --> 00:15:09,239
[SPEAKER_00]: It's from Madhyamakāya 31, the Mahāgauśiṅga Sutta.

186
00:15:10,480 --> 00:15:12,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Aniruddha is living with two other monks.

187
00:15:13,635 --> 00:15:16,316
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha is asking how they're doing.

188
00:15:18,177 --> 00:15:19,117
[SPEAKER_00]: And they each say this.

189
00:15:20,257 --> 00:15:29,920
[SPEAKER_00]: It is a gain for me that I am living with such companions in the holy life and maintain bodily, verbal and mental acts of kindness, both openly and privately.

190
00:15:31,201 --> 00:15:35,882
[SPEAKER_00]: I consider, why should I not set aside what I wish to do and do what these other ones wish to do?

191
00:15:36,402 --> 00:15:38,383
[SPEAKER_00]: We are different in body, but one in mind.

192
00:15:40,340 --> 00:15:42,202
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you make of that last statement?

193
00:15:42,222 --> 00:15:45,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Why should I not set aside what I wish to do and do what these venerables wish to do?

194
00:15:45,686 --> 00:15:54,957
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, we could... you realize that... or the monk there realizes that all three of them are pretty much highly developed.

195
00:15:56,161 --> 00:16:00,703
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it doesn't matter whether my point of view thinks this or his point of view thinks that.

196
00:16:01,123 --> 00:16:02,023
[SPEAKER_02]: We are on the same level.

197
00:16:02,043 --> 00:16:03,804
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it doesn't matter who does what.

198
00:16:04,504 --> 00:16:08,846
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, if we choose to do what he wants, because I could have wanted the same thing, because we are on the same level.

199
00:16:08,906 --> 00:16:11,007
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in that sense, we are just one in the mind.

200
00:16:11,787 --> 00:16:15,889
[SPEAKER_02]: Not one in the mind because, again, they feel this deep spiritual connection.

201
00:16:15,929 --> 00:16:18,710
[SPEAKER_02]: It's one in the mind because they share the same level of wisdom.

202
00:16:20,231 --> 00:16:23,392
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means they invested the same amount of effort and the same amount of work.

203
00:16:23,937 --> 00:16:25,498
[SPEAKER_02]: And got the same amount of results.

204
00:16:25,678 --> 00:16:26,539
[SPEAKER_02]: And they recognize that.

205
00:16:26,599 --> 00:16:28,040
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why it's such a blessing as well.

206
00:16:28,580 --> 00:16:29,421
[SPEAKER_02]: And easy abiding.

207
00:16:30,742 --> 00:16:33,424
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even if they don't even speak, they understand where they are.

208
00:16:34,064 --> 00:16:36,726
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, no mystical intuition or anything like that.

209
00:16:36,766 --> 00:16:38,828
[SPEAKER_02]: It's simply they understood each other's extents.

210
00:16:39,797 --> 00:16:39,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

211
00:16:39,977 --> 00:16:43,079
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't have those problems that come with those kinds of imbalances.

212
00:16:43,179 --> 00:16:44,260
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, of course, yeah.

213
00:16:44,921 --> 00:16:51,605
[SPEAKER_02]: So, so, you know, externally, you cannot necessarily tell apart, but internally, having understood each other, they know exactly where they are at.

214
00:16:52,126 --> 00:16:53,867
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why, yes, it doesn't matter.

215
00:16:53,887 --> 00:16:55,648
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I want to do this, he wants to do that.

216
00:16:55,668 --> 00:16:56,169
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter.

217
00:16:56,209 --> 00:16:58,150
[SPEAKER_02]: It's coming from the same level of the same mind.

218
00:16:59,991 --> 00:17:01,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they were all arhants, I think.

219
00:17:01,713 --> 00:17:01,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

220
00:17:01,993 --> 00:17:03,774
[SPEAKER_00]: Many samādhi attainments and such.

221
00:17:04,495 --> 00:17:04,795
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

222
00:17:06,243 --> 00:17:12,268
[SPEAKER_02]: And you see, like, the communal getting together was only when there was a purpose for it.

223
00:17:12,608 --> 00:17:18,633
[SPEAKER_02]: Preparing for the meal, or cleaning after the meal, or waiting for Pindapad to distribute the food, and so on.

224
00:17:18,733 --> 00:17:23,217
[SPEAKER_02]: It wasn't like, hey, let's get together just for the sake of company.

225
00:17:25,819 --> 00:17:29,883
[SPEAKER_00]: It goes on to say that, like they're saying, like, there's actually two examples.

226
00:17:30,283 --> 00:17:32,485
[SPEAKER_00]: One occurs here, the other I'll mention after.

227
00:17:33,408 --> 00:17:42,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, something along the lines of, like, whoever should see a vessel for drinking water, or a vessel for washing water, or for rinsing, void and empty, should set out water.

228
00:17:43,235 --> 00:17:49,700
[SPEAKER_00]: If it is impossible for him to do this, he should set out water by signaling with his hand, having invited a companion to help him by movement of his hand.

229
00:17:50,261 --> 00:17:54,944
[SPEAKER_00]: But he should not, for that reason, break out into speech.

230
00:17:54,984 --> 00:17:59,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Same thing for various kinds of duties that they would have to do at their little place where they live.

231
00:17:59,868 --> 00:18:00,228
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, see?

232
00:18:00,428 --> 00:18:00,889
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean.

233
00:18:00,909 --> 00:18:01,109
[SPEAKER_02]: Like,

234
00:18:03,313 --> 00:18:04,134
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't do solitude.

235
00:18:04,674 --> 00:18:06,375
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, company is justified.

236
00:18:06,936 --> 00:18:11,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Once company is justified, getting together for all sorts of reasons becomes justified.

237
00:18:12,340 --> 00:18:16,363
[SPEAKER_02]: Once company is justified, getting together for all sorts of reasons is justified, speaking is justified.

238
00:18:17,584 --> 00:18:18,105
[SPEAKER_02]: Actually, it isn't.

239
00:18:18,165 --> 00:18:22,668
[SPEAKER_02]: It's all completely different things, different decisions you make on top of each other.

240
00:18:22,708 --> 00:18:24,830
[SPEAKER_02]: It's more like you should look at it the opposite way.

241
00:18:25,508 --> 00:18:25,688
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

242
00:18:26,049 --> 00:18:28,451
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not ready for the solitude, but I must be ready for the solitude.

243
00:18:29,071 --> 00:18:31,794
[SPEAKER_02]: Until I'm ready for the solitude, sometimes we have to come together.

244
00:18:32,214 --> 00:18:34,697
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, at least when we come together, at least let us not speak.

245
00:18:35,598 --> 00:18:43,385
[SPEAKER_02]: If we have to come together, and if we have to speak, well, at least speak only with the purpose of something practical needs to be said, or dhamma discussed.

246
00:18:44,590 --> 00:18:55,399
[SPEAKER_02]: So, instead of just justifying it further, you still want to, like, make it harder and harder to drift away from the original purpose of being alone and not speaking, in order to understand the dhamma.

247
00:18:55,759 --> 00:19:02,164
[SPEAKER_02]: It's similar, like, when the Buddha instructed, eh... on his deathbed, when he asked, you know, how should we regard women?

248
00:19:03,225 --> 00:19:04,446
[SPEAKER_02]: And he said, don't look at them.

249
00:19:05,747 --> 00:19:07,629
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, if you have to look at them, well, then don't talk to them.

250
00:19:08,293 --> 00:19:15,518
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, if you have to look at them and talk to them, well, then be mindful of the, you know, why you became a monk and sense restraint and everything else.

251
00:19:15,538 --> 00:19:21,863
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's, again, as I said, it's like, okay, so I have to do this, thus everything else that could come out of it is also justifiable.

252
00:19:21,923 --> 00:19:29,089
[SPEAKER_02]: So, we have to come together, so we might as well just talk and address other things and end up finding new reasons for coming up together.

253
00:19:30,950 --> 00:19:31,350
[SPEAKER_02]: And no.

254
00:19:31,490 --> 00:19:34,733
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you are not ready for the solitude, doesn't mean you're justified in company.

255
00:19:39,488 --> 00:19:48,553
[SPEAKER_00]: In the end, like... but every five days, we, those three monks, sit together all night discussing dhamma.

256
00:19:49,054 --> 00:19:56,498
[SPEAKER_00]: The alternative versions in the vinaya, it has the exact same thing, except their purpose is just never talk to each other.

257
00:19:56,558 --> 00:20:02,461
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha chastises them as being like goats, basically, living not actually content and happy.

258
00:20:02,521 --> 00:20:03,022
[SPEAKER_00]: It's almost like...

259
00:20:04,162 --> 00:20:09,487
[SPEAKER_00]: You can, like, how people, like, I don't know, like, live with people they hate, just by avoiding them and let go or getting along.

260
00:20:09,527 --> 00:20:11,028
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, sure, sure.

261
00:20:12,709 --> 00:20:22,817
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it does seem like, yeah, like, it's almost, it seems almost like, like, yeah, if you're gonna live in company, you should use the company for, like, actual beneficial things, not squander it in that sense.

262
00:20:22,998 --> 00:20:29,082
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly, and it doesn't, and that is, you can also then conclude from the same point is, so, it doesn't mean any company.

263
00:20:31,085 --> 00:20:37,387
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you know, even monks, they can be vastly different, coming from vastly different backgrounds, with vastly different views on what the practice is.

264
00:20:38,787 --> 00:20:42,348
[SPEAKER_02]: And some of it is more compatible, some of it... and not all of them want to learn equally.

265
00:20:43,789 --> 00:20:48,350
[SPEAKER_02]: So that doesn't mean you can just... or because you wear a robe... As I said, if the occasion comes,

266
00:20:49,037 --> 00:20:52,478
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, there is the whole etiquette, the whole seniority, which is exactly the purpose of that.

267
00:20:52,578 --> 00:20:53,939
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there is no friction, nothing.

268
00:20:53,959 --> 00:20:56,140
[SPEAKER_02]: You just do it professionally, follow along.

269
00:20:56,780 --> 00:21:01,642
[SPEAKER_02]: But we're talking about actually living with somebody, in someone's proximity, getting together, doing things.

270
00:21:02,102 --> 00:21:11,106
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you realize it requires wisdom and discernment internally, for your own sake, to be able to pass, to be able to discern this rightly, externally.

271
00:21:12,375 --> 00:21:16,637
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not just, oh, whoever you're bundled with, just, you know, make it work.

272
00:21:16,977 --> 00:21:17,898
[SPEAKER_02]: Right, sure.

273
00:21:18,498 --> 00:21:19,458
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't work like that.

274
00:21:19,859 --> 00:21:22,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you shouldn't be bundled with anyone, to begin with.

275
00:21:23,900 --> 00:21:33,085
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, if you do have to be bundled with somebody, because you're not ready for a solitude with the other, you don't have the right view, you're not your own guide, as they say, well, then, bundled with somebody, they will enable you in that.

276
00:21:34,282 --> 00:21:42,992
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like, oh, this, you know, I get on with him, but he's equally ignorant as I am, and we just randomly step in the dark, thinking maybe one of us will stumble upon the dhamma.

277
00:21:44,234 --> 00:21:45,074
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, good luck with that.

278
00:21:46,483 --> 00:21:55,009
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, in that sense, fundamentally, like, when it says, you know, the word kalyāṇamitta, I think you've said this much before, it's always going to be an ārya-sāvaka, so to put it, you know.

279
00:21:55,829 --> 00:21:56,870
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no, like, even a...

280
00:21:57,410 --> 00:22:00,913
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, if you have none, a very wholesome patujana is better than unwholesome.

281
00:22:00,993 --> 00:22:01,493
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

282
00:22:01,533 --> 00:22:02,314
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the, the other...

283
00:22:02,494 --> 00:22:06,617
[SPEAKER_02]: So, they quoted, like, oh, to learn the virtue and generosity from somebody else.

284
00:22:06,657 --> 00:22:07,077
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

285
00:22:07,397 --> 00:22:10,419
[SPEAKER_02]: But don't confuse virtue and generosity for the practice of dhamma.

286
00:22:11,700 --> 00:22:12,140
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a whole

287
00:22:16,490 --> 00:22:17,190
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's the thing.

288
00:22:18,211 --> 00:22:24,695
[SPEAKER_02]: You recognize you're not ready for seclusion, find a friend that will make you ready for seclusion.

289
00:22:25,896 --> 00:22:27,577
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, how should one then...

290
00:22:28,920 --> 00:22:34,982
[SPEAKER_00]: We've mentioned, this has been mentioned before, but like, you know, one can be in seclusion amidst company.

291
00:22:35,422 --> 00:22:44,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Like how the Buddha says, like a practicing monk, even when people visit him, all kinds of important people, his mind inclines still towards that seclusion.

292
00:22:44,364 --> 00:22:46,425
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you won't arrive at that through company.

293
00:22:48,145 --> 00:22:48,545
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean.

294
00:22:48,565 --> 00:22:53,067
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, your mind still has to be, as the Buddha said, inclining towards seclusion and renunciation.

295
00:22:53,647 --> 00:22:57,788
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can only develop the mind inclining in that direction by doing it.

296
00:22:58,832 --> 00:23:07,418
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, once you've been doing it, once that becomes your mode of being, that seclusion, then, yes, people, kings turning up is not a disturbance, because the mind is still in that seclusion.

297
00:23:07,818 --> 00:23:17,445
[SPEAKER_02]: But you will not arrive at that by never actually doing it, by staying in the company, and then justifying your kind of need of company through saying, see, I can become independent.

298
00:23:17,745 --> 00:23:27,912
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like similar somebody who is not restrained from sensuality, is justifying their non-restraint of sensuality, and they can still practice the dhamma, and then the result of the dhamma will somehow restrain them of sensual

299
00:23:28,360 --> 00:23:28,500
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

300
00:23:28,880 --> 00:23:32,641
[SPEAKER_02]: Results of the dhamma are results of restraint of sensuality, not other way around.

301
00:23:34,561 --> 00:23:35,261
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this is the same.

302
00:23:35,281 --> 00:23:39,222
[SPEAKER_02]: Your mind will incline to seclusion as a result of you practicing the seclusion.

303
00:23:40,642 --> 00:23:42,863
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, yes, even the company will not disturb it.

304
00:23:43,883 --> 00:23:53,125
[SPEAKER_02]: So, theoretically, everybody is able to develop that, but you cannot do it on the basis of the lifestyle that contradicts it.

305
00:23:54,449 --> 00:23:56,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it just becomes your new normal, in that sense.

306
00:23:56,491 --> 00:23:58,312
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it becomes... well, it becomes an excuse.

307
00:23:59,313 --> 00:23:59,653
[SPEAKER_02]: You know.

308
00:23:59,713 --> 00:24:02,935
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, well, see, the Buddha said you can be alone in the company.

309
00:24:02,975 --> 00:24:03,876
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, I shouldn't...

310
00:24:03,936 --> 00:24:05,397
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't have to leave my company.

311
00:24:06,158 --> 00:24:10,241
[SPEAKER_02]: You can be alone in the company through being alone first.

312
00:24:10,681 --> 00:24:14,784
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, he wouldn't say the virtue and sense restraint and seclusion comes first.

313
00:24:15,304 --> 00:24:17,186
[SPEAKER_02]: But he did say that a million times.

314
00:24:18,600 --> 00:24:29,001
[SPEAKER_02]: Even in the actual... the first sutta you quoted on Kalanamitā, he said, so how did... what does this friendship really... oh, results in them understanding and practicing seclusion and getting the right view, as a result of their friendship.

315
00:24:31,565 --> 00:24:35,027
[SPEAKER_00]: There was one story that interested me, like in the Parinibbāna Sutta.

316
00:24:35,787 --> 00:24:41,070
[SPEAKER_00]: It said, like, you know, when the Buddha was passing away, or he already passed away, I don't remember.

317
00:24:41,751 --> 00:24:44,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Ananda, he was only a sotāpanna at the time.

318
00:24:44,852 --> 00:24:47,614
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd been that for many, many years.

319
00:24:48,354 --> 00:24:54,378
[SPEAKER_00]: But even then, like, he broke down and, you know, said that kind of stanza where it says, like, my mind that lost the dhamma... Oh, it was the Sariputta who died.

320
00:24:55,190 --> 00:24:55,570
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, was it?

321
00:24:55,630 --> 00:24:55,870
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

322
00:24:55,890 --> 00:24:56,471
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't remember.

323
00:24:56,571 --> 00:24:58,012
[SPEAKER_02]: He was aware of Sariputta died.

324
00:24:58,452 --> 00:25:00,453
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, oh, my mind is like clouded.

325
00:25:00,473 --> 00:25:02,554
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like the biggest branch of the tree dying.

326
00:25:02,574 --> 00:25:03,754
[SPEAKER_02]: Like dullness and drowness.

327
00:25:03,814 --> 00:25:04,055
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

328
00:25:04,355 --> 00:25:10,538
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, in that sense, it sounded like he, like, maybe he hadn't fully even clarified, like, his relationship towards Sariputta in a way.

329
00:25:10,618 --> 00:25:11,298
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously not.

330
00:25:11,438 --> 00:25:14,580
[SPEAKER_02]: No, because, because in the same sense, it says those monks who were arahants,

331
00:25:15,060 --> 00:25:15,921
[SPEAKER_02]: They were not grieving.

332
00:25:15,961 --> 00:25:18,462
[SPEAKER_02]: They said, how can that which has arisen not cease?

333
00:25:19,243 --> 00:25:27,728
[SPEAKER_02]: It was just an absolute matter of fact, that people will die and you, like, having feelings about it is inconceivable, once you fully understood that.

334
00:25:28,628 --> 00:25:31,910
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the fact that he had feelings about it means he hasn't fully understood exactly that.

335
00:25:32,531 --> 00:25:33,752
[SPEAKER_02]: Company in relation with it.

336
00:25:39,955 --> 00:25:42,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you two have anything to bring up or mention?

337
00:25:42,557 --> 00:25:44,698
[SPEAKER_00]: That was most of what I had to bring.

338
00:25:48,760 --> 00:25:57,565
[SPEAKER_01]: The only thing I could think of was that, like, often metta is translated as loving-kindness, but it's friendship.

339
00:25:57,745 --> 00:25:58,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Friendliness.

340
00:25:58,486 --> 00:26:00,487
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same.

341
00:26:00,507 --> 00:26:02,788
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it means non-harm.

342
00:26:03,989 --> 00:26:06,170
[SPEAKER_02]: It means you're unable to have your will.

343
00:26:08,298 --> 00:26:11,560
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't mean, you know, smarmy, wishful thinking.

344
00:26:11,720 --> 00:26:11,981
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

345
00:26:13,301 --> 00:26:15,103
[SPEAKER_00]: And it could very well mean leaving someone alone.

346
00:26:15,123 --> 00:26:16,784
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your most friendly thing to do.

347
00:26:17,124 --> 00:26:17,344
Yeah.

348
00:26:18,065 --> 00:26:18,745
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, in a way, yeah.

349
00:26:19,386 --> 00:26:21,367
[SPEAKER_02]: Non-interfering, non-obstructing.

350
00:26:24,629 --> 00:26:31,694
[SPEAKER_02]: But, you know, when the Buddha talks about compassion and so on, it's... many times he describes what kind of compassion that is.

351
00:26:32,578 --> 00:26:33,759
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a compassion of a doctor.

352
00:26:33,980 --> 00:26:34,600
[SPEAKER_02]: They can help you.

353
00:26:34,961 --> 00:26:40,867
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a compassion of a of a parent that sticks his hand into the throat of his child to pull the thing he's choking on.

354
00:26:41,328 --> 00:26:44,732
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, he'll rip his throat up and you make him bleed, but he'll save his life.

355
00:26:45,573 --> 00:26:46,994
[SPEAKER_02]: And, with the said, that's exactly what he does.

356
00:26:49,057 --> 00:26:50,038
[SPEAKER_02]: So, so that's what I mean.

357
00:26:50,058 --> 00:26:53,542
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you need to start using the criteria for both friendship

358
00:26:54,373 --> 00:27:04,979
[SPEAKER_02]: Instruction not based on how it makes you feel, but after, like, heavily invested effort in discerning, is this beneficial in the long run, which is better for me?

359
00:27:05,699 --> 00:27:17,066
[SPEAKER_02]: Being pleased now with inspirational verses from some spiritual poet, or being told, you know, the truth that might slightly offend me, but deep down I know already, yeah, it makes more sense.

360
00:27:19,132 --> 00:27:25,378
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in other words, you will not have a spiritual friend, kalenamita, in the correct sense, unless you start developing wisdom for yourself.

361
00:27:25,698 --> 00:27:29,161
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you have no criteria for what a true friend is.

362
00:27:29,181 --> 00:27:31,023
[SPEAKER_02]: You might have met them and you got offended by them.

363
00:27:33,745 --> 00:27:36,348
[SPEAKER_02]: And then the ones that make you feel good and tell you what you want to hear, well...

364
00:27:37,247 --> 00:27:38,008
[SPEAKER_02]: They're not your friends.

365
00:27:38,528 --> 00:27:47,616
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a funny siddha, the one about, like, some guy visits the Buddha, and the Buddha tells him, like, you know, suffering is born from those who are dear, but then he doesn't like that.

366
00:27:47,636 --> 00:27:48,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Children are the root of suffering, yeah.

367
00:27:48,937 --> 00:27:52,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and so he goes to some gamblers and, you know, these are my friends, I like that.

368
00:27:52,501 --> 00:27:55,563
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, because they agreed with him, yeah, yeah, exactly.

369
00:27:55,984 --> 00:27:57,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I remember that.

370
00:27:59,066 --> 00:28:03,630
[SPEAKER_02]: He just went to verify, you know, to people they don't tell him, but he wants to hear.

371
00:28:06,066 --> 00:28:21,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, in my own practice, a lot of times, it's been like, you know, kind of hearing that you've been overlooking something, it hurts, but it's like, wow, if I hadn't, like, been, like, poked in that way, like, it's almost like a relief in the back of your mind, but there's that opportunity to be, like, rejected.

372
00:28:21,501 --> 00:28:22,862
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what your mind wants to do.

373
00:28:22,922 --> 00:28:27,667
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like, yeah, you don't even realize how lucky.

374
00:28:28,884 --> 00:28:30,505
[SPEAKER_02]: You are, that you don't have to figure it out yourself.

375
00:28:31,786 --> 00:28:36,730
[SPEAKER_02]: Like the fact that, oh, yeah, I might not want to hear it, but then you realize, ah, that is exactly.

376
00:28:36,790 --> 00:28:38,651
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if I undo this now, then I see it.

377
00:28:38,671 --> 00:28:40,953
[SPEAKER_02]: And that was always the main problem, not seeing where the problem is.

378
00:28:41,413 --> 00:28:44,576
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, and how long would you have gone until you kind of solved where the problem was?

379
00:28:44,596 --> 00:28:45,436
[SPEAKER_02]: Could be decades.

380
00:28:45,456 --> 00:28:46,137
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

381
00:28:47,097 --> 00:28:48,318
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it can be for the rest of your life.

382
00:28:48,378 --> 00:28:48,619
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, never.

383
00:28:48,639 --> 00:28:49,039
[SPEAKER_02]: If you don't see it.

384
00:28:49,059 --> 00:28:50,400
[SPEAKER_02]: Never see it.

385
00:28:50,420 --> 00:28:52,802
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you cannot know what you don't see.

